```
=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router1
diagnosis_text:
The fault is not a physical link failure. Both router1:eth0 and router2:eth0 are
UP/UP at the kernel level with their correct IP addresses (193.10.11.1/24 and
193.10.11.2/24 respectively), so no re-cabling is needed or warranted.

The real root cause is that the FRR routing daemon suite on router1 has crashed
and is in a hard-failed state (systemctl reports "Active: failed"). Because FRR
is down, bgpd is not running on router1, and the router holds no configuration
beyond its directly connected routes. There is no BGP session between AS 1
(router1) and AS 2 (router2), so router1 has no route to 200.1.1.0/24 (pc2's
network) and router2 has no route to 195.11.14.0/24 (pc1's network). When pc1
sends traffic to 200.1.1.2, router1 immediately returns an ICMP "Destination
Net Unreachable" because the prefix is absent from its FIB — matching the
symptom exactly. Router2 confirms the outage: its BGP neighbor 193.10.11.1 is
stuck in state Active, with one prior session established and then dropped
(the moment FRR died on router1), and it is periodically retrying the TCP
connection to port 179 with no success.

Corrective fix: run `systemctl restart frr` on router1. Once FRR restarts,
bgpd will reload the persisted configuration, re-establish the eBGP session
with router2, exchange prefixes, and full pc1↔pc2 connectivity will be
restored within one BGP keepalive cycle (≤60 seconds). No physical
intervention is required.
=== END ===
